有大规模的公民不服从运动。到了80年代末，进入90年代，这基本上使南非变得几乎无法治理。我们谈到了体育抵制，但我认为经济制裁实际上瘫痪了南非经济，不是立即生效，但它像是一个缓慢放气的轮胎。南非商界阶层能感觉到生命力正从经济中流失。是的。

20
00:14:56.000 --> 00:15:21.000
And I think the cultural sanctions actually are massively important as well because they create this sense of isolation, a sense that the rest of the world hates you. Remember that song in the, um, a hysterical song in the 1980s? I've never met a nice South African. Yeah. I mean, there was a sense that South Africa, I think, was having been a sort of promised land for emigrants in their white imagination in the 20th century. Mrs. Thatcher's son, Tony Gregg, a host of people.
我认为文化制裁也非常重要，因为它们创造了一种孤立感，一种全世界都讨厌你的感觉。还记得80年代那首歇斯底里的歌吗？《我从未见过一个好南非人》（I've never met a nice South African）。是的。我的意思是，有一种感觉，南非在20世纪白人的想象中曾经是移民的应许之地。撒切尔夫人的儿子、托尼·格雷格，还有许多人。

21
00:15:21.000 --> 00:15:49.000
It becomes such a pariah that that cannot help but have a corrosive effect on Afriana's sense of self-worth, which goes to your next point, Tom, which is about their... They lose self-confidence. Yeah, exactly. And they lose moral self-confidence. And I wrote about this in Dominion. I was amazed the degree to which kind of theological anxieties about whether apartheid was part of God's plan actually played a crucial role in deflating the self-confidence of the
它变成了一个如此受排斥的国家，这不可避免地对阿非利卡人的自我价值感产生了腐蚀作用，这引出了你的下一个观点，汤姆，关于他们的……他们失去了自信。是的，正是如此。他们失去了道德自信。我在《多米尼恩》（Dominion）中写过这一点，我惊讶地发现，关于种族隔离是否是上帝计划一部分的神学焦虑，实际上在削弱种族隔离政权的自信方面发挥了关键作用，

22
00:15:49.000 --> 00:16:25.000
apartheid regime because so many leading members of the South African government were very devout Christians. And they started to worry that actually apartheid was, as the rest of the world was saying, a kind of mortal sin. You know, basically they, it all kind of, you know, theologically deflated as well as economically and deflated in terms of law and order. But even so, they still might have decided to hold on. I mean, they're a massive minority. They have been suppressing the black majority for decades and decades and decades. They must be nervous about what their fate might be if they give up power.
因为南非政府的许多领导成员都是非常虔诚的基督徒。他们开始担心，实际上种族隔离正如世界其他地方所说的那样，是一种“弥天大罪”。你知道，基本上，他们在神学上、经济上以及法律和秩序方面都崩溃了。但即便如此，他们可能还是决定坚持下去。我的意思是，他们是一个庞大的少数群体。他们几十年来一直在压迫黑人多数派。如果他们放弃权力，他们肯定会担心自己的命运。

23
00:16:25.000 --> 00:17:21.000
And so I think that even with everything that is going wrong for them, they might still have tried to maintain apartheid. Had it not been for the fact that there existed a black leader with whom the leaders of the apartheidians of the apartheidate, Partite regime could contemplate doing business. And this black leader is, of course, one of the most famous political figures of the late 20th century, and that is Nelson Mandela. By far the most formidable, the most celebrated of all the revolutionaries that the campaign against apartheid throws up in South Africa, a member of an OSA-speaking royal family. He becomes the deputy president of the African National Congress and the African National Congress, the ANC, is the leading organization in the struggle against the white rule, and Mandela founds the spear of the nation, which is in effect the armed wing of the ANC.
所以我认为，即使一切都在恶化，他们可能还是会试图维持种族隔离。如果不是因为存在一位黑人领袖，而种族隔离政权的领导人可以考虑与他进行谈判。这位黑人领袖当然是20世纪末最著名的政治人物之一，那就是纳尔逊·曼德拉。他是南非反种族隔离运动中涌现出的最强大、最受赞誉的革命者，出身于一个讲科萨语的皇室家族。他成为非洲人国民大会（ANC）的副主席，而非洲人国民大会是反抗白人统治的领导组织，曼德拉创立了“民族之矛”（Spear of the Nation），这实际上是ANC的武装派别。

24
00:17:21.000 --> 00:18:04.000
And in his kind of career as the leader of the spear of the nation, he became famous for his ability to evade attempts to arrest him. And he becomes fated in the world's press as the black Pimpernel. So he finally arrested in 1962, probably as a result of a tip-off from the CIA. He was convicted of criminal sabotage in 1964 and he gets sentenced to life imprisonment. Gives a great speech as his trial, really moving, rousing speech. Yeah, and so when he goes into prison, he is globally accepted as not just the kind of political, but the moral leader of the campaign against apartheid. And perhaps because of that, initially, he's kept in very brutal conditions.
在他作为“民族之矛”领导人的职业生涯中，他以逃避逮捕的能力而闻名。他在世界媒体中被誉为“黑色红花侠”（Black Pimpernel）。他最终在1962年被捕，很可能是由于中央情报局（CIA）的告密。他在1964年因刑事破坏罪被定罪，并被判处终身监禁。他在审判时发表了一场伟大的演讲，非常感人、激昂的演讲。是的，所以当他入狱时，他被全球公认为不仅是政治领袖，更是反种族隔离运动的道德领袖。也许正因为如此，起初他被关押在非常残酷的条件下。

25
00:18:04.000 --> 00:19:13.000
So he's kept in a tiny cell. He has to wash in icy cold sea water. He's put to hard labour in a stone quarry and the glare of the sun in the quarry is so bright that it permanently damages his eyesight. So very tough conditions. And throughout this period, he never renounces violence. In 1985, the South African government say, look, we'll let you go free if you will renounce violence. And he retorts, only free men can negotiate. Prisoners cannot enter into contracts. he ends up being kept in prison for 27 years, and over the course of those years, he reflects on the limitations of violence. And this, I think, is in large part because of the spiritual, the ideological wellsprings that he's drawing on. So he'd been raised a Methodist. His faith is discreet, but committed, I think. In prison, he very rarely misses a service. He's reading the Bible all the time and he takes seriously what he reads in the Gospels. So the kind of incredibly subversive words of Jesus in the Gospels, love your enemies, and pray for those who persecute you. And when at last,
他被关在一个狭小的牢房里。他必须用冰冷的海水洗澡。他被派去石矿场从事苦役，矿场里阳光的刺眼程度足以永久性地损害他的视力。所以条件非常艰苦。在整个期间，他从未放弃暴力。1985年，南非政府说，看，如果你放弃暴力，我们就让你自由。他反驳道：“只有自由人才能谈判。囚犯不能签订契约。”他最终被关押了27年，在这些年里，他反思了暴力的局限性。我认为这在很大程度上是因为他所汲取的精神和意识形态源泉。他从小是卫理公会教徒。他的信仰是低调但坚定的。在监狱里，他很少错过礼拜。他一直在读圣经，并认真对待他在福音书中读到的内容。所以福音书中耶稣那些极其颠覆性的话语，“爱你的敌人，为那些迫害你的人祈祷”。当最终，

26
00:19:13.000 --> 00:20:02.000
on the 11th of February in 1990, he is freed from prison. He's 71. Amazingly, he returns to the world, determined not just to reclaim his freedom, but to do so in a spirit that is devoid of hatred and bitterness. And he's holding to this philosophy not just as a kind of spiritual discipline, although I think that is absolutely what it is for him, but also because he is a true statesman and he sees this philosophy as the surest way to bind up the kind of the terrible wounds that have been afflicting South Africa for so long. And in short, his aim is to meet the people who had kept him in prison for so long and brutalized his people and discriminated against them. deprived them of every opportunity that they could. He's determined to meet them with forgiveness
在1990年2月11日，他从监狱中获释时。他已经71岁了。令人惊奇的是，他回到这个世界，决心不仅要夺回自己的自由，而且要以一种没有仇恨和痛苦的精神去做。他坚持这种哲学，不仅是作为一种精神纪律，尽管我认为对他来说绝对是这样，而且还因为他是一位真正的政治家，他认为这种哲学是弥合长期以来折磨南非的那些可怕伤口的最佳方式。简而言之，他的目标是去见那些长期将他关在监狱里、残害他的人民、歧视他们、剥夺他们一切机会的人。他决心以宽恕去面对他们，

27
00:20:02.000 --> 00:20:45.000
and he is trusting that they will answer this forgiveness with a mood of repentance. Right. And this essentially is where the Rugby World Cup comes in. So South Africa had been out in the cold in rugby for a long time and more than a decade, which was a massive blow to Afrikan's sense of self-confidence, I think. But it had been readmitted. in 1992 and then awarded the World Cup as a sort of, as a gesture, I guess, you're back in the fold. So they were given the World Cup in 93. And then 1994, they have their first multiracial election, don't they? Properly democratic election.
并相信他们会以悔改的态度来回应这种宽恕。没错。这基本上就是橄榄球世界杯的切入点。南非在橄榄球界被冷落了很长时间，超过十年，我认为这对阿非利卡人的自信心是一个巨大的打击。但它在1992年被重新接纳，然后被授予世界杯主办权，这算是一种姿态，我想，你回来了。所以他们在93年获得了世界杯主办权。然后在1994年，他们进行了第一次多种族选举，不是吗？真正民主的选举。

28
00:20:45.000 --> 00:21:13.000
Yeah, so they get the World Cup before the election. Yeah. But in the expectation, of course, that things are going to change politically in South Africa, which they do, because the African National Congress, Mandela's party, wins a, landslide majority in the first election. And Mandela becomes the first black president. And his relationship with rugby and the way in which he uses it is actually a really, really interesting example of the political use of sports.
是的，他们在选举前获得了世界杯。是的。但当然是基于南非政治将会发生变化的预期，事实也确实如此，因为曼德拉所在的非洲人国民大会在第一次选举中赢得了压倒性多数。曼德拉成为了第一位黑人总统。他与橄榄球的关系以及他利用橄榄球的方式，实际上是体育政治用途的一个非常、非常有趣的例子。

29
00:21:13.000 --> 00:21:44.000
Yeah, because South Africa would never have been given the Rugby World Cup, had Mandela and the ANC not essentially given it the green light even before they become the government. And as you say, Mandela has picked up on the significance that rugby plays in the kind of Afrikaner soul. And by the way, you can't emphasize enough how unusual this is because black South Africans did not, by and large, support the Springbok rugby team. Their sport was not rugby. It was football.
是的，因为如果曼德拉和非国大在他们成为政府之前没有给予绿灯，南非永远不会获得橄榄球世界杯。正如你所说，曼德拉已经意识到了橄榄球在阿非利卡人灵魂中所扮演的重要意义。顺便说一句，你无法过度强调这有多么不寻常，因为南非黑人总体上并不支持跳羚橄榄球队。他们的运动不是橄榄球。是足球。

30
00:21:44.000 --> 00:22:21.000
Rugby was the game of the oppressor. So for Mandela to have any interest in this at all is groundbreaking, it's stunning. Well, it's part of a kind of broader program of self-education that Mandela puts himself through in prison, to try, basically to try and understand the enemy. So he is learning Afrikaans. He's learning about the history of the Afrikaners, their heroes, their mythology, you know, how they understand their role in Africa. Because of course, by this point, they have been in Africa for centuries and centuries. So they are essentially an African people. And Mandela absolutely kind of recognizes that, that they have to be part of the settlement
橄榄球是压迫者的游戏。所以曼德拉对此有任何兴趣都是开创性的，令人震惊的。嗯，这是曼德拉在狱中对自己进行的一种更广泛的自我教育计划的一部分，基本上是为了试图了解敌人。所以他在学习南非荷兰语。他在学习阿非利卡人的历史、他们的英雄、他们的神话，你知道，他们如何理解自己在非洲的角色。因为当然，到这个时候，他们已经在非洲生活了几个世纪。所以他们本质上是一个非洲民族。曼德拉绝对认可这一点，即他们必须成为曼德拉希望达成的和解的一部分。

31
00:22:21.000 --> 00:23:03.000
that Mandela is hoping will come. And so as well as studying African people, history, he also tries to befriend his jailers. He treats them with respect in the expectation, which increasingly the longer he's in prison comes to be met, that the jailers will treat him with respect in return. And he ends up great friends in particular with one guard, Christo Brand. And it's Brand who in the late 1980s starts to educate Mandela in rugby. As you said, it's not just about the rules, it's not just about the players, it's about the role that it plays in Africana culture. And so Mandela comes out of prison entirely understanding how potentially significant rugby might be.
因此，除了研究非洲人民和历史外，他还试图与他的狱卒交朋友。他以尊重的态度对待他们，并期待着——随着他在监狱里待的时间越长，这种期待就越能得到满足——狱卒们也会以同样的尊重来回报他。他最终与一名狱卒克里斯托·布兰德（Christo Brand）成为了好朋友。正是布兰德在80年代末开始向曼德拉普及橄榄球知识。正如你所说，这不仅仅是关于规则，不仅仅是关于球员，而是关于它在阿非利卡文化中扮演的角色。所以曼德拉出狱时，完全理解橄榄球可能具有的重大意义。

32
00:23:03.000 --> 00:23:52.000
And he sees, I think, the World Cup as a way of securing two separate goals. So the first is that it might help win the support of Afrikaners, who are the most inveterate opponents of accommodation with black majority rule. It might help them kind of accept this new multiracial settlement. As you said, for most black Africans, rugby is the archetypal sport of the white oppressor, the Afrikaner oppressor. And so radicals in the ANC, they want to see, for instance, the distinctive green jersey of the Springboks band. They want to see the name Springbox band as well. And they push this demand and Mandela flatly refuses. Mandela has also made a point of reaching out to the captain of the Springbox, who,
我认为他将世界杯视为实现两个独立目标的途径。首先，它可能有助于赢得阿非利卡人的支持，他们是与黑人多数统治达成妥协的最顽固的反对者。这可能有助于他们接受这种新的多种族和解。正如你所说，对于大多数南非黑人来说，橄榄球是白人压迫者、阿非利卡压迫者的原型运动。因此，非国大内部的激进分子希望看到，例如，跳羚队独特的绿色球衣被禁止。他们也希望看到“跳羚”这个名字被禁止。他们推动这一要求，但曼德拉断然拒绝。曼德拉还特意接触了跳羚队的队长，

